,-2.5,0,4,5,5.5]},{"gable":[40,-6.5,9,4.5,13,2.5],"axis":"y","m":"limestone"},{"box":[43.5,-2.5,0,0.5,5,5],"m":"timber"}],"notes":"The H\u00f4tel Sal\u00e9 (Jean Boullier de Bourges, 1656-59), from published descriptions (the museum's own history of the building, Paris-promeneurs): the majestic portal on rue de Thorigny (east) opens into the court of honour, slightly oval and rounded on the entrance side, bordered by low wings with terrace roofs and stone balustrades; at its back the three-storey corps de logis under its tall slate roof with dormers and chimneys, the small avant-corps on the court front carrying the great semicircular pediment; the wider garden front framed by projecting pavilions; the garden (Jardin L\u00e9onor-Fini) to the west. Refined 29 Sep 2026 (no photographs reachable): the h\u00f4tel's walls made pale dressed limestone and its roofs blue-grey slate (they were grey-brown stone and near-black); the Marais neighbours either side, which were two long blank slabs, broken into ten separate houses of different widths and heights under slate roofs, in plaster, stone and pale brick, with tall French windows, and kept lower than the h\u00f4tel's cornice so they frame it rather than wall it in. Guessed: all dimensions, the window rhythm, the roof's height, which way the axis runs (taken east-west), the neighbours' heights."}